\subsection{Iterative Self-Training Pipeline}
\label{sec:pipeline}

We study diversity dynamics through an iterative self-training loop where each generation produces training data for the next. The base model is Qwen3-0.6B \cite{placeholder_qwen3} (596M parameters), chosen as the smallest model capable of coherent instruction-following while permitting complete multi-generation experiments in tractable compute budgets. We use UltraFeedback \cite{placeholder_ultrafeedback} as the prompt source, sampling 2{,}200 general instruction-following prompts (2{,}000 for training, 200 held out for evaluation). Each generation proceeds in four steps: (1) generate $K$ candidate responses per prompt from the current model; (2) score all candidates with ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1 \cite{placeholder_armorm}, a reward model that does not participate in training; (3) select the top-1 candidate per prompt according to ArmoRM scores; (4) fine-tune the base model on the selected subset to produce the next-generation model. We run three complete generations (Gen~0 $\rightarrow$ Gen~1 $\rightarrow$ Gen~2), where each generation comprises the full four-step cycle. Gen~0 metrics reflect the model after the first round of self-training, not the unmodified base model; the variation in Gen~0 Vendi Scores across conditions (105.42 to 108.08) confirms that even the first training round produces configuration-dependent diversity levels. All candidate generation uses temperature $T{=}0.7$ to maintain output diversity within each candidate set.

\subsection{Experimental Conditions}
\label{sec:conditions}

Table~\ref{tab:conditions} summarizes the eight configurations. Our design isolates four factors through controlled comparisons. First, adaptation method (LoRA vs.\ Full Fine-Tuning) isolates the effect of parameter capacity: LoRA \cite{placeholder_lora} updates only 1.67\% of parameters (rank 16), while Full-FT (Full Fine-Tuning) updates all parameters. Second, candidate pool size ($K \in \{4, 8, 16\}$) isolates selection pressure, corresponding to 25\%, 12.5\%, and 6.25\% retention rates respectively. Third, learning rate (5e-6 vs.\ 2e-5 for Full-FT) isolates learning intensity, spanning a conservative to aggressive optimization range. Fourth, curation strategy (ArmoRM top-1 vs.\ random selection) isolates the causal effect of reward-based filtering: configurations C5, C6, and C7 serve as matched baselines for C1, C3, and C4 respectively, differing only in whether selection is reward-guided or random.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Experimental configurations. Each curated condition (C1--C4, C8) has a matched random baseline (C5--C7) that differs only in selection strategy. C8 tests extreme selection pressure ($K{=}16$). Trainable \% refers to the fraction of model parameters updated during fine-tuning.}
\label{tab:conditions}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lllcll@{}}
\toprule
ID & Adaptation & $K$ & LR & Curation & Trainable \\
\midrule
C1 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 4 & 2e-4 & ArmoRM top-1 & 1.67\% \\
C2 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 8 & 2e-4 & ArmoRM top-1 & 1.67\% \\
C3 & Full-FT & 4 & 5e-6 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\% \\
C4 & Full-FT & 4 & 2e-5 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\% \\
C8 & Full-FT & 16 & 2e-5 & ArmoRM top-1 & 100\% \\
\midrule
C5 & LoRA ($r{=}16$) & 4 & 2e-4 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 1.67\% \\
C6 & Full-FT & 4 & 5e-6 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 100\% \\
C7 & Full-FT & 4 & 2e-5 & Random 1-of-$K$ & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

This factorial structure enables direct attribution. If reward curation causes diversity collapse, curated conditions (C1--C4, C8) should show steeper diversity decline than their random counterparts (C5--C7) across generations. If parameter capacity mediates collapse, LoRA and Full-FT conditions should diverge even under identical curation. If selection pressure drives collapse, C8 ($K{=}16$, 6.25\% retention) should collapse faster than C2 ($K{=}8$) and C1 ($K{=}4$).

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}

We evaluate diversity at two levels: semantic content and surface format.

\paragraph{Semantic diversity.} Our primary metric is the Vendi Score \cite{placeholder_vendi}, a kernel-based diversity measure computed over sentence embeddings from all-MiniLM-L6-v2 \cite{placeholder_minilm}. The Vendi Score equals the exponential of the Shannon entropy of the eigenvalues of the kernel matrix, with higher values indicating greater diversity. We complement this with Self-BLEU \cite{placeholder_selfbleu}, defined as the average pairwise BLEU score among all responses in the evaluation set (lower indicates more diversity), and Nearest-Neighbor Distance (NND), the average cosine distance to the nearest neighbor in embedding space (higher indicates more diversity). All semantic metrics are computed over the 200 evaluation prompts with one response per prompt per generation.

\paragraph{Format diversity.} Standard embedding-based metrics can miss surface-level homogenization because semantically distinct responses may share identical templates. We introduce two format-level indicators. Opening Entropy measures the Shannon entropy of first-10-token sequences across responses; lower values indicate convergence toward fixed opening patterns. Near-Duplicate Start Rate measures the fraction of response pairs sharing a 50-character prefix; higher values indicate template repetition. These metrics capture a distinct failure mode: models that produce varied content but wrap it in repetitive formatting.

\paragraph{Quality and training dynamics.} We track mean ArmoRM score per generation to verify that quality does not degrade, and per-generation training loss to monitor learning dynamics and detect underfitting or overfitting.

\subsection{Training Details}
\label{sec:training_details}

For LoRA configurations, we set rank to 16, scaling factor $\alpha{=}32$, and apply adapters to all linear layers. For Full-FT configurations, all parameters are updated without freezing. Both use AdamW \cite{placeholder_adamw} with cosine learning rate scheduling and train for 2 epochs per generation (3 epochs for the aggressive Full-FT setting C4 when loss has not converged). The effective batch size is 16 for LoRA (batch size 4, gradient accumulation 4) and 8 for Full-FT (batch size 1, gradient accumulation 8). All experiments run on 2$\times$ NVIDIA RTX 5090 GPUs (32GB each). The complete pipeline (7 configurations $\times$ 3 generations) requires approximately 48 GPU-hours.

% Section 5: Analysis
% Note: \section{} header is in main.tex

\subsection{Metric-Dependent Collapse: Format vs.\ Semantic}
\label{sec:format_vs_semantic}

Diversity collapse under reward curation is real but mischaracterized by standard embedding-based metrics. Semantic diversity as measured by Vendi Score remains stable across generations (Section~\ref{sec:no_collapse}), yet surface-level formatting undergoes substantial homogenization. Table~\ref{tab:format_collapse} presents format diversity metrics for the LoRA $K{=}8$ curated condition (C2) and the LoRA random baseline (C5) across Gen~0 and Gen~2. Opening unique ratio, defined as the fraction of responses with a unique first 50 characters, drops from 0.701 to 0.367 under curation and from 0.794 to 0.513 under the random baseline. Opening entropy (Shannon entropy over first-10-token sequences, in bits) declines by 1.13 bits under curation versus 0.75 bits under the baseline. The near-duplicate start rate (fraction of responses sharing a 50-character prefix with at least one other response) rises from 50\% to 84\% under curation and from 38\% to 75\% under the baseline. Both conditions converge toward template-like openings, but curation accelerates this convergence and reaches a higher terminal repetition level. The absolute near-duplicate rates reflect different candidate pool sizes ($K{=}8$ vs.\ $K{=}4$); we focus on the generation-over-generation degradation rate within each condition, which controls for this difference.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Format diversity metrics for LoRA $K{=}8$ curated (C2) and LoRA random baseline (C5). Opening unique ratio and opening entropy decline in both conditions; near-duplicate start rate and trigram repetition rate increase. Per-response TTR (type-token ratio) degrades less under curation than under random selection.}
\label{tab:format_collapse}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Metric & C2 Gen~0 & C2 Gen~2 & C5 Gen~0 & C5 Gen~2 \\
\midrule
Opening unique ratio & 0.701 & 0.367 & 0.794 & 0.513 \\
Opening entropy (bits) & 13.09 & 11.96 & 12.43 & 11.68 \\
Near-dup start rate (\%) & 50.0 & 84.0 & 38.0 & 75.0 \\
Per-response TTR & 0.684 & 0.676 & 0.683 & 0.660 \\
Trigram repetition rate & 0.391 & 0.509 & 0.316 & 0.439 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The mechanism behind this format convergence is implicit preference bias in the reward model. ArmoRM assigns higher scores to responses that begin with certain structural patterns (e.g., numbered lists, direct answer statements). Iterative selection amplifies these initially mild biases: a slightly preferred opening template appears more often in the curated training set, the fine-tuned model generates it more frequently in the next generation, and it receives further preferential selection. This feedback loop operates on surface formatting while leaving semantic content largely untouched. Per-response type-token ratio (TTR), which measures lexical diversity within individual responses, drops by only 0.008 under curation compared to 0.023 under random selection. The reward model selects for format templates without constraining the vocabulary or topic coverage within those templates.

The Vendi Score's insensitivity to this phenomenon has a specific technical explanation. The sentence embeddings used for Vendi Score computation (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) encode semantic content and are largely invariant to surface formatting choices. Two responses that begin with identical opening phrases but discuss different topics receive distant embeddings; conversely, two responses on the same topic with different openings receive similar embeddings. The embedding space conflates format-identical but semantically-distinct responses into high diversity, while the format metrics capture a collapse dimension invisible to semantic embeddings. This disconnect means that Vendi Score alone provides an incomplete picture of how iterative training reshapes the output distribution.

\subsection{Why Semantic Collapse Does Not Occur}
\label{sec:why_no_collapse}

Three complementary mechanisms prevent reward curation from narrowing the semantic distribution, even as format diversity declines. First, prompt diversity acts as an external anchor. Our training set contains 2{,}200 diverse prompts spanning different topics, instruction types, and difficulty levels. Even if curation narrows the per-prompt response distribution (selecting one response from $K$ candidates), the cross-prompt diversity is maintained by the prompt distribution itself. A curated training set still contains responses to 2{,}200 distinct questions; semantic breadth at the corpus level is bounded from below by the prompt set's topical coverage. This mechanism operates independently of the curation strategy: as long as the prompt set is diverse, no per-prompt selection rule can eliminate cross-prompt semantic variation.

Second, reward model saturation limits the selection pressure's magnitude. The ArmoRM score gap between the top-1 candidate and the pool average is approximately 0.04 across prompts. This narrow gap means the selected response is not dramatically different from a randomly chosen alternative. In a hypothetical scenario where the reward model assigned scores of 0.9 to one response type and 0.1 to all others, curation would rapidly eliminate low-scoring variants. The observed near-uniform score distribution prevents this collapse pathway. The curated candidate resembles a randomly drawn candidate from the same pool in most respects, differing primarily in format preferences that the reward model weakly favors.

Third, generation stochasticity injects fresh variation at each iteration. We generate candidates at temperature $T{=}0.7$, which ensures substantial randomness in each candidate set. The model does not converge to a single deterministic response per prompt because each generation re-samples from the full output distribution. Even if Gen~1 training data is filtered through reward curation, the Gen~1 model produces a new set of diverse candidates at Gen~2 generation time. The temperature parameter controls the effective width of the candidate distribution and prevents the iterative loop from converging to a fixed point. These three factors (prompt diversity, reward saturation, and generation stochasticity) jointly explain why Vendi Score remains within $\pm$1\% across three curated generations, while the model collapse literature predicts progressive narrowing under data recycling \cite{placeholder_shumailov}. The C8 condition ($K{=}16$, 6.25\% retention) provides the strongest empirical test of these mechanisms. Under four times the selection pressure of our standard $K{=}4$ configurations, the Vendi Score changes by only $-$0.19\% while training loss falls to 0.097, the lowest of any condition. Even when curation retains fewer than 1 in 15 candidates, prompt diversity and generation stochasticity sustain the semantic distribution.

\subsection{LoRA as Capacity Bottleneck}
\label{sec:lora_bottleneck}

The similarity of diversity outcomes between $K{=}4$ and $K{=}8$ LoRA conditions (C1: $-$0.27\%, C2: $+$0.04\%) despite doubled selection pressure suggests that parameter capacity, not curation intensity, is the binding constraint under LoRA. If curation were the primary driver of diversity change, doubling the candidate pool from 4 to 8 (reducing the retention rate from 25\% to 12.5\%) should produce measurably greater diversity suppression. Instead, both conditions converge to nearly identical Gen~2 Vendi Scores (107.25 for both C1 and C2). This convergence is consistent with a capacity-limited regime in which the adapter cannot express the differences between curated and non-curated training signals.

The rank-16 LoRA adapter modifies only 1.67\% of model parameters. All learned changes, whether driven by reward-curated data or random data, must be expressed within this low-dimensional subspace. Training loss provides direct evidence of this constraint: C1 (curated, $K{=}4$) reaches a final loss of 0.166, C2 (curated, $K{=}8$) reaches 0.168, and C5 (random) reaches 0.152. The 0.014 gap between curated and random final loss is small relative to the 0.240 reduction from Gen~0 to Gen~2 within each condition (Table~\ref{tab:training_loss}). The adapter absorbs most of the training signal's structure regardless of whether that signal comes from curated or random selection; the residual difference in loss translates to a negligible difference in diversity outcomes.

This interpretation reframes the 3.40~pp curation gap reported in Section~\ref{sec:direction_reversal}. The gap does not reflect curation actively suppressing diversity. Rather, random data's uncorrelated gradients happen to push the LoRA subspace in directions that slightly increase output diversity, while curated data's correlated gradients (consistent reward-preferred patterns) do not. Under full fine-tuning, where the capacity constraint is removed, this asymmetry disappears and eventually reverses (Section~\ref{sec:direction_reversal}). The practical implication is that LoRA-based studies of reward curation effects should be interpreted cautiously. Observed diversity changes under parameter-efficient fine-tuning may reflect adapter capacity limits rather than properties of the curation strategy itself.

\subsection{Implications for Diversity Measurement}
\label{sec:measurement_implications}

Standard diversity evaluation in iterative self-training relies on embedding-based metrics (Vendi Score, Self-BLEU, nearest-neighbor distance) that are insensitive to format-level homogenization. A typical evaluation workflow computes one or more of these metrics, observes stability, and concludes that diversity is maintained. Our results show that this workflow misses an entire dimension of output collapse. Format diversity metrics reveal progressive homogenization that is invisible to embedding-based evaluation, yet clearly perceptible to human readers who encounter repetitive opening patterns across model outputs.

We propose two complementary metrics for format diversity monitoring in iterative training. Opening entropy measures the Shannon entropy over first-10-token sequences across a response corpus; a declining value indicates convergence toward template openings. Near-duplicate start rate measures the fraction of responses sharing a 50-character prefix with at least one other response; a rising value indicates increasing repetition of opening patterns. Both metrics require only string operations (no embedding model, no GPU), are interpretable without reference distributions, and capture a failure mode orthogonal to what semantic diversity metrics detect. In our experiments, opening entropy drops 1.13 bits while Vendi Score changes by $+$0.04\%, and near-duplicate start rate rises 34 percentage points while Self-BLEU remains below 0.007. These divergent trajectories confirm that the two metric families probe independent dimensions of the output distribution.

The broader lesson is that ``diversity'' in iterative self-training is not a single quantity. Format diversity (how varied the surface structure is), semantic diversity (how varied the topics and arguments are), and lexical diversity (how varied the vocabulary is) can evolve independently under the same training procedure. Our data show that reward curation primarily affects format diversity while leaving semantic and lexical diversity nearly unchanged. A complete diversity audit for iterative training should include at least one metric from each dimension. We note that these observations are drawn from a single model scale (Qwen3-0.6B), reward model (ArmoRM), and prompt distribution (UltraFeedback); the relative sensitivity of these dimensions to curation may differ at other scales or with different reward models.

% Limitations section content below
% Note: \section{} header is in main.tex

Our findings are subject to several scope constraints that bound their generalizability.

\paragraph{Model Scale.}
All experiments use Qwen3-0.6B (596M parameters). Larger models produce higher-quality candidates with greater score variance, which could amplify curation effects in either direction: stronger selection pressure from wider score gaps, or greater resilience from richer internal representations. Whether the semantic stability we observe holds at the 7B or 70B scale remains an open question.

\paragraph{Generation Depth.}
We evaluate three generations of iterative self-training. The model collapse literature typically requires more iterations before distributional narrowing becomes severe \cite{placeholder_shumailov}. Our results establish that reward curation is safe over three generations, but they do not guarantee stability at generation ten or beyond. Subtle compounding effects below our measurement threshold could accumulate over longer horizons.

\paragraph{Domain Coverage.}
The UltraFeedback prompt set (2,200 instructions) covers general instruction-following across diverse topics. Narrow domains such as code generation or mathematical reasoning impose tighter output constraints and may exhibit different collapse dynamics. In particular, domains where correct answers cluster tightly in embedding space could reduce the protective effect of prompt diversity.

\paragraph{Single Reward Model.}
We use ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1 as the sole reward signal throughout all experiments. Different reward models encode different biases: some may penalize structural variety more aggressively, others may exhibit sharper score distributions that strengthen curation pressure. The specific format collapse patterns we document (opening template convergence, near-duplicate starts) reflect ArmoRM's preferences and may not generalize to all reward models.

\paragraph{Format Metric Granularity.}
Opening entropy and near-duplicate start rate capture homogenization in the first 10 tokens and 50 characters of each response, respectively. These metrics are blind to structural convergence in middle sections, concluding paragraphs, or organizational patterns such as list usage and paragraph structure. A complete characterization of format collapse would require finer-grained positional analysis that our current metrics do not provide.

